From: Nex package maintainers
Subject: [PATCH] homedir: select the system configuration directory from UAPI paths
Source: no upstream commit; written for Nex against GnuPG 2.5.17
Upstream: not submitted. GnuPG resolves every system configuration file through
 one compiled directory, so a distribution cannot ship a default that a boot or
 an administrator may replace. The change below adds the generic sysconfdir,
 /run, and libdir order, keeps the administrator's directory as the highest
 priority one, and names no Nex path, so any Linux system that follows the UAPI
 configuration files specification can use it.

gnupg_sysconfdir() returned the single compiled GNUPG_SYSCONFDIR, and every
component hands that one directory to libgpg-error with
gpgrt_set_confdir (GPGRT_CONFDIR_SYS, ...), while agent/trustlist.c and
agent/genkey.c build filenames from it directly. gpg.conf, gpg-agent.conf,
dirmngr.conf, gpgsm.conf, scdaemon.conf, common.conf, gpgconf.conf,
trustlist.txt, and qualified.txt therefore had exactly one system location. A
package could not ship a default below its own libdir, and a boot-time tool had
nowhere to put a temporary one.

Try the administrator's directory, then /run/gnupg, then the package's own
libdir, and use the first that exists. Because the option parser takes one
directory rather than a search path, the choice is made per directory rather
than per file: the highest tier that exists supplies all of them. That is
coarser than a per-file rule, but it is what the single-directory interface
allows, and it makes the package and boot tiers reachable at all, which they
previously were not. When no directory exists the administrator's path is
still returned, so a component keeps its old behaviour of finding no system
configuration. The directory is resolved on each call, so gpg-agent picks up a
newly created directory on SIGHUP. An explicit gpgconf.ctl relocation still
wins over all three.

The /run directory is written literally rather than derived from a new
automake macro, because the release tarball ships generated Makefile.in files
that already embed the -D flags; adding one to am/cmacros.am would require
regenerating the build system for no gain.

diff -urN a/common/homedir.c b/common/homedir.c
--- a/common/homedir.c
+++ b/common/homedir.c
@@ -1486,6 +1486,35 @@
 /* Return the name of the sysconfdir.  This is a static string.  This
    function is required because under Windows we can't simply compile
    it in.  */
+#ifndef HAVE_W32_SYSTEM
+/* Return the system configuration directory that is in force.  The three
+   directories are tried in decreasing priority: the administrator's
+   directory, then a directory that one boot owns, then the directory the
+   installed package ships.  The first one that exists supplies every system
+   configuration file, because the option parser takes a single directory
+   rather than a search path.  When none exists the administrator's directory
+   is returned, which keeps the previous behaviour of quietly finding no
+   system configuration.  The directory is resolved on each call rather than
+   cached, so a reload sees a directory that an administrator has just
+   created.  */
+static const char *
+sysconf_dir_in_force (void)
+{
+  static const char *const dirs[] = { GNUPG_SYSCONFDIR,
+                                      "/run/gnupg",
+                                      GNUPG_LIBDIR };
+  struct stat sb;
+  int i;
+
+  for (i = 0; i < DIM (dirs); i++)
+    if (!stat (dirs[i], &sb) && S_ISDIR (sb.st_mode))
+      return dirs[i];
+
+  return GNUPG_SYSCONFDIR;
+}
+#endif /*!HAVE_W32_SYSTEM*/
+
+
 const char *
 gnupg_sysconfdir (void)
 {
@@ -1504,7 +1533,7 @@
   if (dir)
     return dir;
   else
-    return GNUPG_SYSCONFDIR;
+    return sysconf_dir_in_force ();
 #endif /*!HAVE_W32_SYSTEM*/
 }
 
